==== Incompleteness Analysis of Search and Filtering Features

:author: Nelson Caban

===== Introduction

The Lost & Found system includes search and filtering features that help users find reports related to lost or found items. These features are important because users may not remember the exact category or tag assigned to an item. They may instead search using words from the title or description of a report.

This lecture topic task applies Domain Analysis and Concept Formation by analyzing the current search and filtering behavior for incompletenesses. In this case, the focus is on identifying missing search behavior in the Lost & Found report discovery process.

===== Current Domain Description Units

The following domain description units describe the current search and filtering behavior:

* A user can search for reports using the search bar.
* A report can contain tags.
* A report can contain a title.
* A report can contain a description.
* A report can belong to a category.
* A report can have a status, such as Active, Claimed, or Closed.
* The system filters reports by status.
* The system filters reports by category.
* The system filters reports by the user's search query.
* The previous search behavior focused on matching the search query with report tags.

These description units show that the system has several pieces of report information available. However, if the search bar only checks tags, then the system does not fully use all available report information for discovery.

===== Identified Incompleteness

The main incompleteness is that the search bar can be described as a general report discovery tool, but its behavior may only search through tags. This creates an incomplete search experience because reports also contain titles and descriptions that may include important searchable information.

For example, a report may have the title "Black Hydro Flask" and the description "Found near the library entrance." If the report does not include a tag such as "bottle" or "library," a user searching for "Hydro Flask" or "library" may not find the report if the search only checks tags.

This shows an incompleteness in the domain behavior:

* The system has searchable information in the title.
* The system has searchable information in the description.
* The system has searchable information in the tags.
* The search bar should support report discovery.
* However, the search bar may only use tags.

Therefore, the search behavior is incomplete because it does not fully use the searchable attributes available in a report.

===== Concept Formation

The incomplete behavior can be improved by forming a higher-level concept called searchable report attributes.

.Searchable Report Attribute Concepts
[cols="1,1,1", options="header"]
|===
| Report Field
| Domain Concept
| Search Purpose

| Title
| Primary item identifier
| Helps users search by the main name or label of the item

| Description
| Detailed item information
| Helps users search using details, context, or item characteristics

| Tags
| Keyword metadata
| Helps users search using assigned labels or keywords

| Category
| Classification constraint
| Helps users narrow results by item type

| Status
| Report state constraint
| Helps users narrow results by report lifecycle state
|===

By grouping title, description, and tags under the concept of searchable report attributes, the system can support a more complete search model.

===== Improved Domain Narrative

A user enters a search query to discover reports that may match a lost or found item. The system treats the report title, description, and tags as searchable report attributes. The system compares the user's query against these searchable attributes while also respecting the selected report state and category constraints. Reports that match the search query and satisfy the active filters are displayed to the user as report summaries.

This improved narrative removes the incompleteness found in the earlier behavior because the search bar is no longer limited to tag metadata. Instead, it searches across the main report information that users are most likely to remember.

===== Recommended Solution

The recommended solution is to keep the existing tag search functionality while extending the search bar to also search through report titles and descriptions.

The search logic should check whether the user's search query matches any of the following:

* Report title
* Report description
* Report tags

The system should also preserve the existing status and category filters. This means that a report should only appear if it matches the active status tab, matches the selected category filter, and matches the search query when a query is provided.

This approach improves the completeness of the search feature without removing previous functionality.

===== Validation Scenarios

The following scenarios can be used to validate the improved behavior:

* If a user searches for a word found in a report title, the report should appear.
* If a user searches for a word found in a report description, the report should appear.
* If a user searches for a word found in a report tag, the report should still appear.
* If the search bar is empty, the system should continue showing reports based on the selected status and category filters.
* If a report does not match the selected status, it should not appear even if the search query matches.
* If a report does not match the selected category, it should not appear unless the category filter is set to all categories.
* Existing tag search functionality should continue working after title and description search are added.

===== Analysis

This incompleteness analysis shows that the search feature should not only depend on tags. Tags are useful, but they are not always complete or predictable. Users may search using natural words from the title or description instead of exact tags assigned to the report. By expanding the search bar to include title and description, the system better supports real user behavior.

This also improves the domain model because title, description, and tags can now be understood as part of the same higher-level concept: searchable report attributes. This abstraction makes the system easier to document, maintain, and extend in the future.

===== Conclusion

The Lost & Found search and filtering system had an incomplete discovery behavior when the search bar only considered tags. Since reports also contain titles and descriptions, those fields should be included as searchable report attributes. Extending the search functionality to include title, description, and tags creates a more complete and useful report discovery process while preserving the existing filtering structure.

